WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:30.000
JEFFREY EISENACH: Welcome, everyone, to AEI.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.440
My name is Jeff Eisenach.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.860
I’m a visiting scholar here, and I’m delighted
to welcome everyone to today’s discussion

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:45.080
with the European Union’s commissioner for
competition, Margrethe Vestager.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.489
Before introducing Commissioner Vestager,
I’d like to note that we have probably several

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:53.970
luminaries in the room today, but, in particular,
Ambassador of the European Union to the United

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.839
States David O’Sullivan is here.

00:00:55.839 --> 00:01:01.460
I wanted to welcome Ambassador O’Sullivan
and thank him for being here.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.840
On a procedural note, our program will begin
today with remarks from Commissioner Vestager.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.950
After that, she’s agreed to take some questions.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.890
I’ll ask the first few, and then we will
open it up to the audience.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.430
When we do, we’ll ask that you wait for
the microphone and that you state your name

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.690
and your affiliation before asking your question.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:28.740
Now, it’s a great honor for us to have Commissioner
Vestager with us today.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:35.159
She’s a native of Denmark and has served
in very senior positions in the Danish government,

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:40.650
including as minister of education from 1998
through 2001, minister for economic affairs

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.880
in the interior from 2011 to 2014.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.860
She was political leader of the Danish Social
Liberal Party.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:54.880
Ambassador O’Sullivan and I were talking
about those words have somewhat different

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.579
meanings in Denmark maybe than they do here.

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:05.549
But she was political leader of the Danish
Social Liberal Party from 2007 to 2014.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:11.030
And it’s my understanding that she has served
as the role model for the protagonist of a

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.760
popular Danish television series about the
election of the country’s first female prime

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.980
minister.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:23.680
So she’s not prime minister, but someone
plays one based on her on television.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.690
In Brussels, she served as president of the
Economic and Financial Council, also known

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:36.290
as ECOFIN, in 2011, and in October 2014, she
was confirmed by the European Parliament as

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.750
commissioner for competition.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:43.330
Since then, she has been responsible for some
of the largest and most significant antitrust

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.070
actions in the history of the European Union.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:52.770
Well, not all of these, and indeed, the majority
have not involved US companies.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.730
Certainly the ones that have gotten the most
attention on this side of the Atlantic have,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:03.560
including the abuse of dominance finding against
Google earlier this year, resulting in a fine,

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:09.670
the largest antitrust fine in European Union
history of $2.7 billion.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:15.760
And her 2016 finding that Ireland’s tax
treatment of Apple has violated the EU’s

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:26.210
prohibitions against state aid, meaning that
Apple owes currently $14 billion in back taxes.

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:30.670
Actions like these have won praise from some
and criticism from others.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:38.020
Here at AEI, we are committed to free enterprise
and competitive markets, including competition

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.690
in the marketplace of ideas.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:46.030
And with that in mind, it’s an honor to
welcome Commissioner Vestager to the American

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.030
Enterprise Institute.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.940
We look forward to your remarks.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.100
(Applause.)

00:03:52.100 --> 00:04:05.110
MARGRETHE VESTAGER: Ladies and gentlemen,
it is a — it’s a great pleasure to be

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.640
here.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:13.760
And I knew I’d feel welcome, and it’s
not only because of the very sort of Danish

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:20.000
way my name was pronounced, which is actually
quite rare, I can tell you this.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:29.090
But also that this institute has a motto:
The competition of ideas is fundamental to

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.370
a free society.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:38.340
And for me, this is what policymaking is all
about.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.710
If our ideas are never challenged, they’ll
never get any better.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:52.819
We end up stuck with ideas that once served
us well but haven’t kept up with changes

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:56.770
in our world and our societies.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:02.189
And when we don’t hear different views,
well, I think also we lose the freedom to

00:05:02.189 --> 00:05:06.050
think for ourselves.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:16.020
And just as competition of ideas is fundamental
to a free society, competition in our markets

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:27.449
is fundamental to a fair one because challenges
and competition makes us better.

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:34.460
If businesses are not challenged, if they
don’t have to compete, well, they don’t

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:41.550
have any reason to work better to serve people
better.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:48.469
Competition is that motor that actually drives
businesses to do better for consumers, to

00:05:48.469 --> 00:05:57.740
cut prices, to offer more choice, to produce
innovative products.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.840
Competition enforcers didn’t build that
motor.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:12.819
We just help to keep it running, running smoothly
by getting rid of the obstacles that may stop

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:17.340
competition from doing its work.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:25.659
And this is why antitrust rules prevent cartels,
stop dominant companies misusing their powers

00:06:25.659 --> 00:06:29.360
to drive out competitors.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:36.550
This is why we have rules to make sure that
mergers do not undermine competition.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:43.789
And this is also why, now, more than 60 years
ago, when the European Union was founded,

00:06:43.789 --> 00:06:51.939
we were given rules on state aid to stop governments
using subsidies to give some companies an

00:06:51.939 --> 00:07:00.389
unfair benefit and, therefore, unravel the
level playing field.

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:05.460
The most basic aim of competition rules is
that of keeping prices down.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:14.830
In the past five years, more than half of
antitrust decisions has been about putting

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:23.849
a stop to cartels, like our decision last
year to fine five European truck makers a

00:07:23.849 --> 00:07:34.069
total of nearly three billion euros for a
cartel that fixed gross list prices of trucks

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:36.039
for 14 years.

00:07:36.039 --> 00:07:43.169
Most of our work on mergers is also about
keeping prices down.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:47.590
In more than
90 percent of the mergers that we have to

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:55.810
step into, the last year and the year before,
our main concerns were that the merger would

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.360
have meant fewer competitors and higher prices.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.669
And that makes a real difference.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:12.650
In those two years, our estimate is that the
work on mergers and cartels has saved customers

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:17.999
as much as 30 billion euros.

00:08:17.999 --> 00:08:24.199
And that is without ever considering the mergers
that were never filed or the cartels that

00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:31.889
were never formed because our rules wouldn’t
have allowed for it.

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:36.560
But there is more to competition than keeping
prices down.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:45.380
Even when a product seems to be free, like
a search engine or a social network, competition

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:49.560
still helps to get consumers a better deal.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:55.339
Last year, we looked at Microsoft’s merger
with LinkedIn.

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:01.800
And even though most people used LinkedIn
without paying for it, at least in cash, we

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.660
were concerned that the merger would affect
competition.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:14.130
We knew that after the merger, the two companies
would connect their products.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:23.259
And that would have risked shutting down other
professional social networks of the European

00:09:23.259 --> 00:09:25.829
market.

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:28.740
Without competition, the user would have lost
choice.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:36.660
They could have lost, for example, the chance
to choose another service or a service with

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:39.930
the best privacy policy.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:44.870
So we only approved the merger after Microsoft
gave us commitment that it would keep the

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:50.300
market open also after the merger.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:58.300
Competition gives us more choice and lower
prices today, but it also helps us to get

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:09.220
better products tomorrow because competition,
the drive to get ahead of your competitors,

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:14.949
that pushes businesses to keep innovating.

00:10:14.949 --> 00:10:21.420
Earlier this year, we found that the merger
between Dow and DuPont would not just raise

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:26.680
prices but also hold back innovation.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:37.500
Dow and DuPont were two of just five companies
that took part in all stages of developing

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:43.100
pesticides to meet the needs of farmers throughout
the world.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:54.060
And we found evidence that they planned to
cut back on their research after the merger.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.740
That would have slowed innovation.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:04.750
It would have left farmers stuck with older
products that may have been less effective

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.019
and more toxic.

00:11:07.019 --> 00:11:14.149
So we only approved the merger after companies
agreed to sell a large part of DuPont’s

00:11:14.149 --> 00:11:22.290
pesticide business, including the worldwide
research arm for pesticides.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:31.270
Innovation was also an issue in our decision
against Google earlier this year.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:36.920
Consumers don’t pay to use Google’s search
engine, not in cash at least.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:45.949
But that doesn’t change the fact that search
of the internet is a market, and it is one

00:11:45.949 --> 00:11:52.120
in which Google is dominant in Europe, with
more than 90 percent market share in most

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.910
European countries.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:02.420
Market dominance gives a special responsibility
— a special responsibility under European

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.639
competition law.

00:12:04.639 --> 00:12:11.440
And this is a responsibility not to abuse
the power but to keep competition only on

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:15.720
the merits of products and services.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:23.649
And in our decision, we established that Google
did not comply with this obligation.

00:12:23.649 --> 00:12:31.250
Instead, Google decided to use its power as
a search engine to deny other companies a

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:39.220
chance to compete on neighboring markets — namely,
the market for shopping comparison.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:46.550
Google showed its own comparison shopping
service at the very top of the first page

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:56.680
of search results, and that gave its own services
a prominence that its competitors couldn’t

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:57.899
match.

00:12:57.899 --> 00:13:04.500
Google also demoted its rival services in
search results so that they only appeared

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.829
on average on page four.

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:15.040
And just go through your recent behavior when
you have done a search on the internet.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.819
How often did you get to page four?

00:13:18.819 --> 00:13:26.029
I see no raised hands.

00:13:26.029 --> 00:13:30.100
The results were effective, impressive.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:35.850
Google’s rivals suddenly lost as much as
90 percent of their traffic.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:42.300
And despite their best efforts, they never
got all these visitors back.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:52.290
And that matters not because we feel sorry
for those competitors.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:59.310
After all, for competition to do its job,
well, companies have to be able to lose as

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:02.230
well as win.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:06.040
But that cuts both ways.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:14.760
Big companies also have to face competition,
to face the risk of failure, and that won’t

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:21.100
happen if we allow them to use their power
to stop anyone else from having the chance

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.110
to compete.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:30.720
Because when companies can’t lose, they
cannot do better either.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:41.529
Of course, and this is obvious — I’m sorry
to waste your time saying this — it is not

00:14:41.529 --> 00:14:48.460
the job of a competition authority to take
the place of the markets to set the prices

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:57.459
of trucks, to tell Google or Dow that innovation
is important and what it can produce.

00:14:57.459 --> 00:15:07.399
But we know that as long as competition stays
fierce, those companies will have no choice

00:15:07.399 --> 00:15:17.139
but to meet consumers’ needs, to keep prices
low, to make an effort to innovate.

00:15:17.139 --> 00:15:24.279
And there is no need for us to interfere in
that process by trying to say precisely what

00:15:24.279 --> 00:15:30.180
should be the outcome of competition.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:37.910
But there are times when public authorities
as such need to go further, when it does make

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:46.649
sense to control what the market produces,
when our health or our environment or our

00:15:46.649 --> 00:15:49.750
basic rights are in question.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:57.720
These are important concerns, but they’re
not something that competition rules can fix

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:07.199
— not because there is a problem with our
competition rules, but because competition

00:16:07.199 --> 00:16:11.029
is only part of the answer.

00:16:11.029 --> 00:16:13.569
Look at pesticides, for example.

00:16:13.569 --> 00:16:22.600
Farmers, they need affordable, innovative
pesticides, and competitive markets can answer

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.490
that.

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:30.689
So by protecting competition, our decisions
on the mergers between Dow and DuPont will

00:16:30.689 --> 00:16:34.560
keep prices down and innovation thriving.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:40.709
And we will take the same approach when we
look at the merger — the planned merger

00:16:40.709 --> 00:16:44.860
between Bayer and Monsanto.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:48.400
But prices and innovation are not the only
things that matter.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:17:00.149
I’ve had many letters, postcards, emails,
tweets from people who are concerned about

00:17:00.149 --> 00:17:04.560
these chemical mergers.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:14.120
Sometimes they worry about prices and innovation,
but ever so often they are worried about what

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:22.930
pesticides can do to the environment or to
our health or the safety of our food.

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:30.260
And I agree that we cannot just rely on the
market to guarantee that pesticides are safe

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:36.909
for the farmers, for environment, for us as
consumers.

00:17:36.909 --> 00:17:44.190
We need the protection of clear and strict
regulation.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:52.140
Europe has some of the world’s most stringent
pesticide regulations in the world.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:18:00.799
Those regulations applied before the mergers,
and they will still apply after the mergers,

00:18:00.799 --> 00:18:08.580
no matter what form they may be completed
in, both to the companies that merge and to

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:12.230
companies that do not merge.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:16.840
But we shouldn’t mix things up.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:23.990
My job as a European commissioner for competition
is to look at how a merger will affect exactly

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:32.100
competition, and that is what I will do with
my teams.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:40.330
Controlling vehicle emissions is another case
where competition law enforcement is not enough.

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:48.300
Understandably, many people are concerned
about how their lifestyle affects the environment.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:54.169
And through the market, they can put a lot
of pressure on companies to innovate and to

00:18:54.169 --> 00:18:57.940
produce greener products.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:07.059
And if companies sort of answer or respond
to that pressure by working together to resist

00:19:07.059 --> 00:19:12.111
the demand for greener products, then competition
enforcers may have to step in.

00:19:12.111 --> 00:19:19.470
And that’s what happened in this truck-makers
cartel I just told you about because the other

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:24.120
side of it was not only the coordination of
gross list prices.

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.220
It was also that its

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:33.330
members coordinated the introduction of greener
technology so that none of them would bring

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:38.809
the new technology to the market before they
were forced to do that.

00:19:38.809 --> 00:19:44.460
But we cannot just rely on the markets to
protect the environment.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:51.880
We still need rules for vehicle emissions
that protect our health and our climate.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:20:02.370
And we need regulators who can punish companies
that break those rules — but it is for them.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:06.870
Competition and enforcement is a bit of a
paradox.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:12.320
Our job is to help markets work better and
more freely.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:21.820
But to do that, we need to interfere in the
market, and that is, of course, a paradox.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:27.090
And it makes the work a delicate balance.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.630
We shouldn’t step in when competition is
not really threatened.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:41.280
We shouldn’t block a merger, for example,
that would cut costs for consumers, but it

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:47.730
is just as important that we do not look away
when we see something happening that does

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:49.110
harm competition.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:55.309
Either way, if we get it wrong, consumers
will suffer.

00:20:55.309 --> 00:20:58.130
So what can we do?

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:05.399
And this will be the end, because I would
like, of course, to make a bow because I think

00:21:05.399 --> 00:21:11.950
the good thing to do is to look at the motto
of this institute by making sure that also

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:17.820
our ideas of competition are open to competition.

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:23.309
We can exchange ideas with experts both in
Europe and further away.

00:21:23.309 --> 00:21:28.279
And, in fact, we do just that all the time.

00:21:28.279 --> 00:21:33.770
We do it through organizations like the International
Competition Network, which brings together

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:39.649
more than 100 authorities from all over the
world, including the Federal Trade Commission

00:21:39.649 --> 00:21:43.080
and Department of Justice.

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:51.350
And that network helps us to discuss some
of the trickiest issues of competition enforcement,

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:59.300
and it helps us to reach common ground as
we did in 2014, as an example, when we agreed

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:05.900
on recommended practices on predatory pricing.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:10.150
We also exchange ideas about cases.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:18.170
Since the start last year, we worked with
US authorities on more than 20 different mergers,

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:27.420
like the merger between AB InBev and SABMiller,
the world’s two largest beer brewers, or

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:31.210
the ChemChina takeover of Sygenta.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:38.559
We’ve discussed things like how these mergers
might harm consumers and what could be done

00:22:38.559 --> 00:22:42.260
better to restore competition.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:49.659
And that collaboration and those discussions
have helped us to protect consumers better.

00:22:49.659 --> 00:22:56.030
Being open to new ideas is maybe more important
now than ever because our markets are going

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:59.570
through enormous changes.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:01.860
Some of those changes are about technology.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:09.110
Algorithms are taking over decisions from
people, including decisions on the prices

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:12.230
that companies charge.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:22.260
Big data is also a big helper to cut costs
and improve services, which could mean that

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:27.580
companies without that data struggle to compete.

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:35.049
Meanwhile, some markets are showing signs
of becoming more concentrated, with fewer,

00:23:35.049 --> 00:23:44.850
bigger companies, even if so far in Europe
those signs are limited to a few industries

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.169
in certain countries.

00:23:48.169 --> 00:23:55.529
So we need to discuss these issues now, when
we can still adapt.

00:23:55.529 --> 00:24:02.519
And that is why I’m looking to set up a
panel of experts from outside the commission

00:24:02.519 --> 00:24:05.320
to advise us on how these

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:13.470
changes will affect consumers and how competition
enforcement should respond.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:19.269
Because even though our world is changing,
I think it is clear that competition rules

00:24:19.269 --> 00:24:22.100
still matter.

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:27.809
For many, many years to come, competitive
markets will still be the best way to meet

00:24:27.809 --> 00:24:37.289
consumers’ needs — low prices, wide choice,
innovative products for tomorrow.

00:24:37.289 --> 00:24:45.110
And to make sure that markets work, we need
competition rules to be enforced.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:54.390
Applying those rules in practice won’t get
any easier, but we don’t have a choice.

00:24:54.390 --> 00:25:02.070
Competition in the market is as fundamental
to a fair society as competition of ideas

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.990
is to a free one.

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:08.919
And I think that is as true today as it has
ever been.

00:25:08.919 --> 00:25:12.010
Thank you.

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:15.100
(Applause.)

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:18.190
DR.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:31.580
EISENACH: Well, you’ve covered a lot of
ground, and I want to spend a couple of minutes

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:36.110
just drilling down on some of these current
antitrust issues.

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:42.130
And let me start with a quote, kind of very
famous in American antitrust jurisprudence

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:47.630
from I think the judge with the best name
of all time, Learned Hand.

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:52.840
Somehow never made it on the Supreme Court,
but Judge Hand, a very famous Appeals Court

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:59.059
judge, wrote that it was very important that
antitrust not punish the successful competitor,

00:25:59.059 --> 00:26:05.539
the firm which by virtue of its expertise,
its hard work, its entrepreneurship is successful

00:26:05.539 --> 00:26:08.210
in capturing the market.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:13.920
And, as Judge Hand said, the successful competitor,
having been urged to compete, must not be

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.580
turned upon when he wins.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:21.970
So as you think about the problem — that’s
an old quote, and this is an old problem.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:27.110
That’s a quote from 1945 — as you think
about that and you think the Googles of the

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:33.460
world and the Facebooks of the world who unquestionably
have won the race, how do you distinguish

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:40.350
between conduct which is harmful to consumers
and harmful to competition as opposed to conduct

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:47.340
which is simply harming the unsuccessful,
the less — the firms producing less value,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:51.630
who haven’t been as entrepreneurial or successful
in the race?

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:52.630
MS.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:27:00.480
VESTAGER: Well, this is exactly the balance,
to make sure that you get the right distinctions.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:03.570
In Europe, we have no ban on monopolies.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:07.029
You’re more than welcome to grow one yourself.

00:27:07.029 --> 00:27:11.210
You can’t buy one, but you can grow one.

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:20.850
If you have products that people like, and
they keep coming, then you can keep growing.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:27.210
The only thing is that when you then because
of the virtues of your products become dominant,

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:31.360
then you get a special responsibility because
it is quite obvious that competition suffers

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:34.710
if you yourself hold like 90 percent of the
market.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:42.350
So you get this responsibility not to misuse
your muscle.

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:45.659
And this is exactly the thing.

00:27:45.659 --> 00:27:46.659
You can grow.

00:27:46.659 --> 00:27:47.659
You can grow big.

00:27:47.659 --> 00:27:52.279
We will congratulate you all the way, but
the congratulation stops if we find that you

00:27:52.279 --> 00:27:55.049
start misusing your position.

00:27:55.049 --> 00:28:00.080
And this is, of course, what will have to
be tested on in courts because this is exactly

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:01.280
the case of the Google case.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:02.280
DR.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:03.600
EISENACH: Also the Intel case.

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:14.649
So last week — in 2009, the commission found
Intel in violation of the abuse of dominance

00:28:14.649 --> 00:28:21.980
rules by virtue of giving rebates to some
of its customers in a way that was alleged

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.929
to drive out competition.

00:28:23.929 --> 00:28:25.250
And last week,

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:28.470
the European Court of Justice remanded that
decision.

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:32.799
It’s been through the courts now in a couple
of rounds of appeal, remanded the decision

00:28:32.799 --> 00:28:33.990
back to a lower court.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:39.900
And as I read, fully read the decision, but
as I understand it, on the grounds that neither

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:45.880
the commission nor the lower courts had given
sufficient account to the question of whether

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:50.090
Intel’s conduct would harm an equally efficient
competitor.

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:57.260
So that’s a standard which is very much
part of American jurisprudence.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:29:01.309
Do you see that decision having an effect
on — first of all, how do you see that decision

00:29:01.309 --> 00:29:04.720
as it affects the Intel matter, to the extent
you can talk about it?

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:10.120
And then, do you see that decision affecting
other matters underway, Google, Colcom, and

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:11.400
so forth?

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:12.400
MS.

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:16.370
VESTAGER: Well, it’s hard to have an opinion
on something that is undecided.

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:17.370
DR.

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:18.370
EISENACH: Right.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:19.370
MS.

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:23.140
VESTAGER: Because, basically, the European
Court of Justice sets the general court, you

00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:25.570
have to redo your work.

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:32.029
You have to look into the substance once again,
and the rest of us will have to, of course,

00:29:32.029 --> 00:29:35.460
respectfully wait and see what comes out of
this.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:41.490
In the meantime, obviously, we have work to
do, and we have also of course developed working

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:47.500
methods and lines of thinking since the Intel
decision back in 2009.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:51.250
So we will keep working our cases.

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:56.159
And I think it is important also to recognize
that most cases, they will be looked at from

00:29:56.159 --> 00:30:01.230
both sides — from the sides of what’s
the effect and what’s the objects of the

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:02.820
behavior.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:07.549
The two other respects of the case — the
one of jurisdiction was a very clear win for

00:30:07.549 --> 00:30:14.930
the commission, and the third one will sort
of keep us in sort of thinking mode for some

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:24.860
time because it was a question of how a meeting
with a witness was being recorded.

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:27.220
It had no influence on the concrete case.

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:31.950
We have changed procedures since then, but
the court still gave us guidance as how to

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:36.740
do this looking forward, and that, of course,
we will respect and try to follow that guidance.

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:37.800
DR.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.929
EISENACH: OK.

00:30:39.929 --> 00:30:46.539
So I’m sure there are real limits on what
you want to say or can’t say but just with

00:30:46.539 --> 00:30:49.110
respect to Google because, obviously, there’s
a lot of interest.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:53.690
I think we have not had the full written decision
released yet, unless it’s been very recent.

00:30:53.690 --> 00:30:57.750
So when will we see — because there are
a lot of people looking forward to reading

00:30:57.750 --> 00:30:59.630
it.

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:04.019
And then, just for people who are not following
this as closely, maybe can you talk a little

00:31:04.019 --> 00:31:10.289
bit about the concerns with Android and AdSense
and what those investigations can do?

00:31:10.289 --> 00:31:11.289
MS.

00:31:11.289 --> 00:31:12.289
VESTAGER: Yeah.

00:31:12.289 --> 00:31:16.380
We really do our best to try to make our decisions
public as fast as possible.

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:23.500
But that, of course, had to respect that there
can be concerns of business confidentiality

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:25.519
so we have to clear the decisions first.

00:31:25.519 --> 00:31:28.080
And that takes the time it takes.

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:32.740
But that we make a priority after the decision
has been taken because, as you say, lots of

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:37.929
people would like to read the decisions in
full, as full as they can be when we have

00:31:37.929 --> 00:31:44.350
redacted some of the most confidentiality
concerned issues.

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:45.919
You have a very difficult language.

00:31:45.919 --> 00:31:46.919
You realize that?

00:31:46.919 --> 00:31:47.919
(Laughter.)

00:31:47.919 --> 00:31:54.470
I have a number of words, I try to avoid them,
but, well, anyway.

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:56.940
The two other cases are very different.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:03.039
The AdSense case is a case about placement
of ads on third-party sites.

00:32:03.039 --> 00:32:09.390
What can you make out of demands, how this
can be worked for people also want to enter

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:14.360
this market of placing ads on third-party
websites.

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:21.610
The Android case may be more interesting because
it has to do with dominance and search when

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:25.390
we all go mobile because this is, of course,
the only thing that is growing and growing

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:26.440
very, very fast.

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:27.860
We all become mobile.

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:31.500
We have our smartphones, tablets, smaller
screens.

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:39.150
And in the statement of objection, what we’re
saying is that, as we see it, Google has been

00:32:39.150 --> 00:32:42.940
using Android, which is a very interesting
operating system.

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:47.440
It’s open source — lots of amazing things
can be said about that.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:52.149
But it has been used to make sure that when
you open your box, then you have the Google

00:32:52.149 --> 00:32:54.590
experience on your smartphone.

00:32:54.590 --> 00:33:00.290
And now, of course, we’re in the process
of going through the Google defense on the

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:05.740
statement of objection, and eventually we
will come to a decision.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:06.740
DR.

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:07.740
EISENACH: OK.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:12.580
One distinction — or at least I think a
lot of Americans perceive a distinction between

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:14.840
US enforcement and in the EU.

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:19.139
I think this applies both to mergers and maybe
especially abuse of dominance — is the role

00:33:19.139 --> 00:33:20.620
of competitors.

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:25.970
So it’s certainly the case that competitors
will show up with complaints before the Federal

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.260
Trade Commission and the Department of Justice.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:33.559
But I guess at least the perception is that
they receive a friendlier hearing when they

00:33:33.559 --> 00:33:39.100
show up in front of DigComp in Brussels.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:40.509
Do you share that perception?

00:33:40.509 --> 00:33:47.049
Do you think that European antitrust jurisprudence
is less skeptical or gives more weight to

00:33:47.049 --> 00:33:49.870
competitor complaints than in the United States?

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:53.820
And if so, do you think that’s a good thing
or a bad thing?

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:54.820
MS.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:34:00.990
VESTAGER: Well, I don’t think our courts
have this bias, and since they don’t have

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:04.200
the bias, we don’t have the bias.

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:11.149
Because our decisions, they go to court almost
with no exception — a few, but almost with

00:34:11.149 --> 00:34:12.679
no exception.

00:34:12.679 --> 00:34:16.980
So we have to prepare to be heard in court.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:21.970
And what court wants is, of course, the jurisprudence,
is the evidence, the fact of the case.

00:34:21.970 --> 00:34:27.870
They don’t want to hear any sort of soft
feelings, and oh, we feel sorry for you.

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:30.169
It has no place in a courtroom.

00:34:30.169 --> 00:34:39.380
So, yes, of course it’s a source of information,
of concern to get a case started, but it’s

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:41.340
not the case of the competitor.

00:34:41.340 --> 00:34:49.129
It is what happens to the consumer, if this
behavior can actually be proven with the evidence

00:34:49.129 --> 00:34:51.419
of the case.

00:34:51.419 --> 00:34:56.169
And this is what we do, and I don’t think
there’s any problem in listening to complainants,

00:34:56.169 --> 00:34:58.369
taking on board the evidence they come with.

00:34:58.369 --> 00:35:01.490
It’s our responsibility to make sure that
we get the evidence right.

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:02.490
DR.

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:09.280
EISENACH: Well, you mentioned consumer welfare,
and this is my last question or next to last.

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:17.150
You know, we live in obviously very challenging
times, times of tremendous change, political

00:35:17.150 --> 00:35:20.040
change both in the US and the EU.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:24.550
Brexit and the EU, the Trump election

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:30.500
obviously are signs of turmoil in the United
States.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:39.480
And the sense that big business or high tech
has not produced the kind of benefits that

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:42.430
people would have liked to have seen or at
least they haven’t been shared fairly across

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:49.579
society has led to calls on both sides of
the Atlantic for competition to play a more

00:35:49.579 --> 00:35:54.350
active role in remediating those problems.

00:35:54.350 --> 00:36:01.650
You know, you said in talking about the Apple
decision, I think, that part of the motivation

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:08.650
there was that Europeans were angry, I think
was the word you used about what’s happened,

00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:13.900
and about the role playing by kind of big
high-tech companies earning billions in profits

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:16.290
and not playing taxes.

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:20.400
In the US, we’ve had this phenomenon of
what some people have referred to as hipster

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:23.829
antitrust, the notion that antitrust ought
to expand.

00:36:23.829 --> 00:36:29.940
And some of our, we call them progressives
here, senators, have, you know, come up with

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:33.350
the notion that antitrust ought to play a
much broader role in kind of pursuing social

00:36:33.350 --> 00:36:34.350
justice.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:37.780
So I read some of the things you’d written
previously, and you spoke to this issue from

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:42.940
the podium here, taught in that — and I
apologize for the broad question — but in

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:47.810
that kind of broad context, you know, how
do you see the limits of antitrust?

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:53.230
How far can antitrust go to helping to pursue
these kind of social justice objectives, and

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:58.450
what are the hard limits where it just needs
to step back to say, that’s not ours to

00:36:58.450 --> 00:36:59.760
solve?

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:01.070
MS.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:11.450
VESTAGER: Well, for us, we are obliged by
our laws, by our treaty, and those political

00:37:11.450 --> 00:37:16.859
choices were taken a long, long time ago,
60 years ago.

00:37:16.859 --> 00:37:18.829
And this is our mandate.

00:37:18.829 --> 00:37:22.940
And I think it is very, very important to
stick to that mandate because even though

00:37:22.940 --> 00:37:30.470
a lot of competence is with the European Commission,
it’s a very powerful tool.

00:37:30.470 --> 00:37:37.000
I think anyone who has been doing a little
do-it-yourself knows that just because you

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:41.160
have a wonderful hammer, not everything is
a nail.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:45.020
I know firsthand from — you wouldn’t want
to see my walls.

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:53.069
So you definitely have to know the limitations
of antitrust because there are democratically

00:37:53.069 --> 00:37:56.700
elected institutions who have their responsibility.

00:37:56.700 --> 00:38:02.310
And I think it’s a good thing because otherwise
you completely delegitimize what we do in

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:08.640
our analytical work in mergers, in a cartel
investigation, looking into a case of misuse

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:15.540
of dominant position, if we start to pour
other things down that pot.

00:38:15.540 --> 00:38:17.070
And that I think is important.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:22.780
And the other thing is that we also try to
be precise to say, well, when we take a decision,

00:38:22.780 --> 00:38:29.440
it’s about a certain behavior, a certain
behavior that may be illegal because most

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:34.550
companies also have produced wonderful things.

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:40.260
I can remember what it was like to try to
find something on the internet before we had

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:43.560
search engines.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:48.829
You could hardly try because you had to get
the full address completely right, otherwise

00:38:48.829 --> 00:38:50.660
just forget about it.

00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:56.040
The innovation produced by some of the companies,
well, they have changed our societies.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:58.490
They have changed our lives.

00:38:58.490 --> 00:39:01.140
These are amazing innovations.

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:05.089
And this is why they are successful.

00:39:05.089 --> 00:39:08.640
I think if you would ask any European, why
do you Google things?

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:10.619
Why do you have a Facebook account?

00:39:10.619 --> 00:39:12.560
They’d say, it works.

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:13.560
It’s wonderful.

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:14.560
I like it.

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:19.200
They would never come up with the answer that
it’s because it’s an American company.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:20.460
DR.

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:25.079
EISENACH: Well, thank you for all of those
great answers.

00:39:25.079 --> 00:39:27.840
And now we do have an opportunity for the
audience.

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:30.470
I think the first hand I saw was right here.

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:31.470
Wait for a microphone.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:32.680
It’s on its way.

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:35.490
And introduce yourself, please.

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:38.180
Q: Hello, Commissioner.

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:39.750
My name is Don Baker (sp).

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:46.650
I’m a former — spent 10 years in antitrust
enforcement at the Justice Department.

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.990
And I wanted to ask you about these fines.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:57.829
In other words, we in this country tend to
look at big fines as something that we impose

00:39:57.829 --> 00:40:04.880
on conduct that’s unmitigated — you know,
there’s no justification for it, per se

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:09.390
illegal, and we limit it pretty much to cartel
kind of activity.

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:17.810
In Europe, you’re levying record fines against
conduct that in a way falls more within a

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:23.300
rule of reason — namely, priorities and
things like this.

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:31.150
And, by contrast, in this country, we get
injunctive relief, maybe structural relief

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:35.050
against monopolies, but we don’t hammer
them with big fines.

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:46.440
And I just wondered whether you have any reaction
to that kind of issue 

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:47.440
and concern.

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:48.440
MS.

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:54.540
VESTAGER: Well, I never sort of had the line
of thinking that we’re hammering big companies.

00:40:54.540 --> 00:41:01.400
We are punishing illegal behavior because
you can be big in Europe — very, very, very

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:02.880
big.

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:06.910
You can grow enormous market shares if consumers
like your products.

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:16.020
And the level of fines recently is more a
reflection of the size of the companies because

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:23.400
we have a set of guidelines to say what to
take into consideration, turnover, duration,

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:28.859
sort of the depth of the illegal behavior.

00:41:28.859 --> 00:41:33.470
And that, of course, we follow in order for
people to know what they would get themselves

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:39.960
into if they cartelized a product or misused
a dominant position.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:43.460
So, obviously, this is important for us.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:51.280
I don’t know the differences in any detail
between the two jurisdictions, but what I

00:41:51.280 --> 00:42:00.630
can see is that even with the differences,
it still allows us to work very closely together.

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:05.890
What is different is treated differently,
but where things are the same, I also do hope

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:09.790
that companies find that they get more or
less the same treatment.

00:42:09.790 --> 00:42:11.540
Q: I wasn’t suggesting — (off mic).

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:12.540
MS.

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:13.540
VESTAGER: No, no.

00:42:13.540 --> 00:42:14.750
Q: I know fines are turnover based.

00:42:14.750 --> 00:42:15.750
MS.

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:16.750
VESTAGER: Yes.

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:17.750
DR.

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:21.040
EISENACH: Can I follow up on that, because
it raises — that’s a fabulous question,

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:22.170
Don.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:29.369
So we have a way of imposing big fines on
big corporations that violate Section Two.

00:42:29.369 --> 00:42:30.540
We do it through private litigations.

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:33.481
I’m reminded that I think — maybe I

00:42:33.481 --> 00:42:39.010
have this wrong — but I think someone won
$4 billion in the Microsoft case as a result

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:44.690
of private litigation, follow-on litigation
after the government’s prosecution.

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:50.860
So in that context, what is the state of private
antitrust litigation in the European Union,

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:56.040
and the thinking in the context of the Google
matter, for example, presumably some of the

00:42:56.040 --> 00:43:01.260
competitors who have been harmed by Google
as found by the commission?

00:43:01.260 --> 00:43:04.010
Presumably some of them would be pursuing
litigation.

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:05.010
Where does that stand?

00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:09.250
Because it’s never been as big a factor
in the EU as it has been in the US.

00:43:09.250 --> 00:43:10.250
MS.

00:43:10.250 --> 00:43:11.250
VESTAGER: No.

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:15.010
It hasn’t, and it’s only very recently
that our legislation has come into effect

00:43:15.010 --> 00:43:16.480
for private damages.

00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:23.690
And now, the last member states are just in
the process of making it also national legislation.

00:43:23.690 --> 00:43:31.940
We’re seeing sort of the first private damages
sort of coming up, but it’s way too early

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:37.920
to have any kind of prognosis as to how will
this define the future.

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:43.300
The very basic idea — and it was, of course,
by my predecessor, was to say, well, if you

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:50.260
also can go for private damages, the deterrent
effect will be much bigger because we haven’t

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:53.610
had this before, but we’re seeing the effects
in other jurisdictions.

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:54.610
DR.

00:43:54.610 --> 00:43:58.839
EISENACH: We had another question here.

00:43:58.839 --> 00:44:04.220
Q: Hi.

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:05.220
My name is Matt Miller.

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:09.460
I’m with Capital Group, but we run the American
Fund’s family of Mutual Funds.

00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:15.360
And I’m curious — the general privacy
data regulation, which is taking effect I

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:22.270
believe in May in Europe, do you expect that
that will impact the business models of the

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:27.720
large tech firms through what I’ve seen
as discussion of different opt-in or consent

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:35.109
provisions, and how might that impact your
own work in the competition sphere?

00:44:35.109 --> 00:44:36.670
Any thoughts on that?

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:37.790
MS.

00:44:37.790 --> 00:44:42.770
VESTAGER: Well, as you’re suggesting, this
is not my portfolio.

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:47.730
This is in the hands of my colleague, Vera
Jurova.

00:44:47.730 --> 00:44:51.809
But, obviously, this is for us very important.

00:44:51.809 --> 00:44:55.800
A lot of work has gone into making new privacy
rules.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:58.490
They come into effect May next year.

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:04.440
And the main benefit is that you get one set
of rules for the entire European Union.

00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:09.430
There are some things that are new — the
right to be forgotten, portability of your

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:15.640
data — but, basically, it sort of creates
the benefit of having one set of rules.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:23.520
I think to some degree, because my nature
is sort of a glass-half-full nature, it can

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:30.170
be very beneficial because if businesses take
this on board, maybe also to some degree to

00:45:30.170 --> 00:45:37.910
offer privacy by design, it can sort of re-create
or reconnect with users.

00:45:37.910 --> 00:45:43.310
I think the last time Europeans were asked,
four out of five said we feel that we absolutely

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:48.190
have no control of our personal data.

00:45:48.190 --> 00:45:54.400
And I think that poses a risk to all the benefits
that can come from big data.

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:59.750
So if this can be a push to say, well, we
will make it more accessible, more understandable

00:45:59.750 --> 00:46:06.650
so that you know what you’re doing and rules
are simpler, you don’t have to relate to

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:13.130
— as a business to 28 different ways of
doing this, then I think it can be beneficial

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:17.500
both on the business side of things and on
the user side of things.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:19.140
DR.

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:24.050
EISENACH: This gentleman.

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:29.530
Q: My name is Ali Breland.

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:31.850
I’m a reporter with The Hill newspaper.

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:37.839
The question I had was about how White House
officials had reportedly discussed making

00:46:37.839 --> 00:46:40.210
companies like Google and Facebook public
utilities.

00:46:40.210 --> 00:46:48.150
I was curious to get your take on this argument
and see if you’re amenable to it.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:49.150
Thanks.

00:46:49.150 --> 00:46:50.150
MS.

00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:56.020
VESTAGER: We have a very high bar for that.

00:46:56.020 --> 00:47:03.520
Public utility or essential facility, as we
would call it, the obvious examples would

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:11.400
be sewers, railroads, the tracks, that kind
of thing because the test would be that it

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:20.830
is not economically doable to do a parallel
structure, that it’s prohibitive in its

00:47:20.830 --> 00:47:22.079
nature.

00:47:22.079 --> 00:47:27.130
And so far, we have not found that to the
digital part of our economy.

00:47:27.130 --> 00:47:35.640
There you still find that, well, some of these
structures and some of these networks, it

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:40.240
is not the economy that prevents you from
doing something similar.

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:44.570
So this is why I think this discussion has
been slightly different in Europe.

00:47:44.570 --> 00:47:45.570
DR.

00:47:45.570 --> 00:47:53.809
EISENACH: To follow up on that, again, so
there has been discussion about big data as

00:47:53.809 --> 00:48:00.309
an essential facility, if I can take those
two words and put them together, and with

00:48:00.309 --> 00:48:06.990
the proposal not so much being one of public
utility regulation, but rather mandated sharing

00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:08.859
of some sort.

00:48:08.859 --> 00:48:16.510
And I think a year or so ago, the commission
issued — had a consultation on some of these

00:48:16.510 --> 00:48:18.390
questions.

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:23.819
Where did that come out, and what is the current
thinking on kind of the need for companies,

00:48:23.819 --> 00:48:30.490
whether it’s a Google or a Facebook have
unique big-data data sets or arguably, allegedly

00:48:30.490 --> 00:48:32.760
unique big-data data sets?

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:36.330
What is the thinking on whether they should
be required under some circumstances to share

00:48:36.330 --> 00:48:38.370
those with potential competitors?

00:48:38.370 --> 00:48:39.370
MS.

00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:49.160
VESTAGER: Well, we try to make things complicated
by saying that you cannot discuss big data

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:55.319
just as big data because there are many, many,
many different kinds of data.

00:48:55.319 --> 00:49:01.740
On the one hand side, you have data that is
not to be replicated, that has a very long

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:08.260
duration, that effectively can serve as an
asset that can foreclose competitors from

00:49:08.260 --> 00:49:09.540
entering your market.

00:49:09.540 --> 00:49:16.500
On the other hand side, you have data which
is easily recollected, the duration is very

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:21.660
short, it’s very open for competitors to
get the same or more data.

00:49:21.660 --> 00:49:26.960
And then, of course, you have all complex
situations in between.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:32.869
We have had a few cases, not with us, but,
for instance, the French National Competition

00:49:32.869 --> 00:49:39.170
Authority — they ordered I think what was
their old electricity incumbent, who used

00:49:39.170 --> 00:49:44.670
to have a monopoly, to share some of their
customer data with competitors in order to

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:51.890
do sort of add-on sales for those customers
who were buying electricity because you couldn’t

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:53.619
get this data any other way.

00:49:53.619 --> 00:50:01.130
But that being said, I think it is very important
to keep this conversation complicated because

00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:07.840
otherwise we sort of just close down I think
a lot of the opportunities in data when it

00:50:07.840 --> 00:50:14.430
comes to health, transportation, environment,
preventing all kinds of things that we don’t

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:16.109
want in our societies.

00:50:16.109 --> 00:50:23.500
But, obviously, we also prepare ourselves
— as I mentioned the Microsoft-LinkedIn

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:30.369
case — to look into these questions also
in some detail to see if the question of data

00:50:30.369 --> 00:50:35.200
and the form it takes will be a competition
problem.

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:36.320
DR.

00:50:36.320 --> 00:50:37.950
EISENACH: So many hands.

00:50:37.950 --> 00:50:39.540
Why don’t we stay in this row?

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:43.980
This is going to be the lucky row today.

00:50:43.980 --> 00:50:46.380
Q: Lydia Beyoud.

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:49.820
I’m a reporter with the Event Driven News.

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:54.609
Commissioner, can you give us some more details
on the foreign investment screen that the

00:50:54.609 --> 00:50:58.730
commission is considering and whether or not
you think the US’s CFIUS model is something

00:50:58.730 --> 00:51:02.780
that could be adopted in Europe?

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:03.880
MS.

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:10.420
VESTAGER: Yes, we have exactly been looking
into other models because we see that the

00:51:10.420 --> 00:51:12.000
US has this kind of screening.

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:13.880
I think Canada has it as well.

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:15.970
We find it in Japan.

00:51:15.970 --> 00:51:20.920
Some member states already have FDI screening
in different forms.

00:51:20.920 --> 00:51:29.030
And I think since world trade and also investment
is developing quite fast these years, as I

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:36.720
see it, it becomes more qualified, more based
on mutual trust and building up relationships

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:41.079
when it comes to different ways of regulating
issues that takes your interest.

00:51:41.079 --> 00:51:48.829
But I think it is very important that we have
this element of reciprocity — that if you

00:51:48.829 --> 00:51:53.069
can buy things with me, I can buy things with
you.

00:51:53.069 --> 00:51:59.059
And it is completely legitimate to say, well,
we would like to have access to consider if

00:51:59.059 --> 00:52:04.790
this is not against what we could call public
order.

00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:09.250
I know this is a rather broad concept, but,
anyway.

00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:15.560
And, for us, it has been important as well,
again, not to sort of just pour it in the

00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:21.720
merger analysis part because that would just
complicate things there, but to keep it as

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:26.359
a separate instrument now to be discussed
with member states.

00:52:26.359 --> 00:52:30.569
I think the proposal is to be tabled, if it
hasn’t been tabled, very, very recently.

00:52:30.569 --> 00:52:31.569
DR.

00:52:31.569 --> 00:52:36.670
EISENACH: I think we’ll take one last question,
and we’ll go right here in the front row.

00:52:36.670 --> 00:52:42.180
Q: Lou Gagliano, independent consultant.

00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:51.940
The question I have is: How do you look at
the contracts that governments give to corporations,

00:52:51.940 --> 00:53:01.690
evaluate the question of is that a fair contract
or not given the fact that that government,

00:53:01.690 --> 00:53:03.760
whether it’s Ireland or any other member
of the commission?

00:53:03.760 --> 00:53:09.809
The European Community has its own responsibility
to manage its own affairs.

00:53:09.809 --> 00:53:16.790
So how do you really not step on their kind
of ability to run their own country?

00:53:16.790 --> 00:53:17.790
MS.

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:23.130
VESTAGER: Well, we have been looking into
— and this has been going on for quite some

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:24.130
time.

00:53:24.130 --> 00:53:29.110
A number of the cases were started with my
predecessor, and part of the work when it

00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:32.690
comes to fiscal state aid go all the way back
to Mario Monte.

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:40.680
So it’s a long-standing tradition, and our
courts have said very precisely that we have

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:45.710
a responsibility to control state aid no matter
the form it takes.

00:53:45.710 --> 00:53:47.120
It could be cash.

00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:49.520
It could be fiscal benefit.

00:53:49.520 --> 00:53:52.319
It could be a building lot.

00:53:52.319 --> 00:53:58.270
It can be a loan with a very variable interest
rate.

00:53:58.270 --> 00:54:05.010
And in the fiscal state aid cases, we’ve
been looking at both schemes but also tax

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:06.830
rulings.

00:54:06.830 --> 00:54:12.049
And when we look into the tax rulings, they
are by nature selective because this is for

00:54:12.049 --> 00:54:17.280
just one company and then available to anyone
else.

00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:22.760
And then, of course, we see, well, does this
give a selective advantage in the way that

00:54:22.760 --> 00:54:24.210
things are set up?

00:54:24.210 --> 00:54:30.410
And this is our test: Is there selective advantages
here?

00:54:30.410 --> 00:54:36.180
The Irish are perfectly welcome to have a
corporate level of taxation of 12.5 percent

00:54:36.180 --> 00:54:43.319
as well as Luxembourg are perfectly — it’s
perfectly legal for them to have I think 28.

00:54:43.319 --> 00:54:50.099
So you find varied corporate tax rates, but
our test is if it’s for you but not for

00:54:50.099 --> 00:54:52.049
everyone else.

00:54:52.049 --> 00:54:57.410
Some of the schemes that we have found have
been schemes to say, well, this is for multinationals

00:54:57.410 --> 00:55:01.789
only but not for stand-alone businesses, as
the scheme in Belgium.

00:55:01.789 --> 00:55:07.579
And, on the other hand side, we have found
schemes in other countries that were set up

00:55:07.579 --> 00:55:13.500
to promote national businesses to the disadvantage
of multinational businesses.

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:17.230
And those, of course, we have dealt with as
well and in the same way to say, well, you

00:55:17.230 --> 00:55:18.270
cannot do that.

00:55:18.270 --> 00:55:25.789
You have to make sure that the way you use
your freedom to do your own taxation that

00:55:25.789 --> 00:55:27.650
it’s done with a level playing field.

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:28.650
DR.

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:32.760
EISENACH: We’re going to let that be the
last comment, level playing field.

00:55:32.760 --> 00:55:33.760
MS.

00:55:33.760 --> 00:55:34.859
VESTAGER: Level playing field, that’s a
beautiful word to put us on.

00:55:34.859 --> 00:55:35.859
DR.

00:55:35.859 --> 00:55:36.859
EISENACH: Thank you so much for being here.

00:55:36.859 --> 00:55:37.859
MS.

00:55:37.859 --> 00:55:38.859
VESTAGER: It was a pleasure.

00:55:38.859 --> 00:55:39.859
Thank you very much.

00:55:39.859 --> 00:55:39.860
(Applause.)

